        "content": "# Declarative Pipeline — Failure Handling and Recovery\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Declarative Pipeline** in Stage 03: Jenkins Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.\n\nDeclarative Pipeline provides a structured syntax for defining Jenkins automation. Its pipeline block organizes agent selection, options, parameters, environment variables, stages, parallel branches, and post conditions. The structured model improves readability and allows Jenkins to validate much of the pipeline syntax before execution. Teams should keep stage responsibilities clear and use controlled error handling instead of hiding failures.\n\nExplain how pipeline-as-code defines repeatable delivery behavior. Describe the Jenkinsfile location, pipeline syntax, agent declaration, stages, steps, environment, parameters, options, post conditions, and relevant Groovy behavior. Compare structured Declarative Pipeline with the flexibility of Scripted Pipeline where appropriate. Explain source-control review, syntax validation, maintainability, and safe handling of pipeline logic.\n\nAfter studying this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Jenkins components involved, configure or execute the relevant operation, inspect its result, and recognize the risks of incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Technical Behavior and Component Responsibilities\n\nJenkins is an automation server. The controller manages configuration, job scheduling, pipeline orchestration, plugins, credentials integration, and build metadata. Agents execute build steps in their workspaces. Executors represent the available execution slots on a node. A pipeline coordinates multiple operations and records their results.\n\nA Jenkins build can interact with source-control systems, build tools, test frameworks, code-quality platforms, container registries, artifact repositories, and deployment environments. Each integr